<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Government Museum, Mathura</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mathura Museum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Government Museum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, was founded in 1874 and holds one of the finest collections of early Indian sculpture. Its galleries are rich in Kushan and Gupta works of the Mathura school, including early images of the Buddha and the headless statue of the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a> found at the dynastic sanctuary of Mat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/government-museum-mathura.jpg" alt="Government Museum, Mathura" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Biswarup Ganguly · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Government_Museum_-_Mathura_2013-02-23_5014.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
